WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.670
Before we start,
let's look at what

2
00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:05.595
the ML practitioner
term refers to.

3
00:00:05.595 --> 00:00:08.190
Many users within
an organization

4
00:00:08.190 --> 00:00:10.755
have a part to play
in the ML lifecycle.

5
00:00:10.755 --> 00:00:13.035
For example, a product manager

6
00:00:13.035 --> 00:00:14.580
who simply types of query to

7
00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:16.350
pull necessary insights from

8
00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:18.630
a data dashboard
or data warehouse.

9
00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:21.240
Or a data scientist who works on

10
00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:24.510
different aspects of building
and validating models.

11
00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:28.230
Think also about an ML engineer
who is responsible for

12
00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:30.180
the model to work
without issues to serve

13
00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:32.400
end-users in a
production environment.

14
00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:34.905
ML practitioner is
used to describe

15
00:00:34.905 --> 00:00:38.365
all these different roles
throughout the ML lifecycle.

16
00:00:38.365 --> 00:00:42.230
Now, let's look at the challenges
these ML practitioners

17
00:00:42.230 --> 00:00:44.180
face when they
operationalize and

18
00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:46.660
make their models
available for production.

19
00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:49.010
These challenges
include managing

20
00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:51.365
and keeping track
of complex details,

21
00:00:51.365 --> 00:00:53.030
such as data, model

22
00:00:53.030 --> 00:00:56.620
architectures, hyperparameters,
and experiments.

23
00:00:56.620 --> 00:00:58.975
As for specific pinpoints,

24
00:00:58.975 --> 00:01:01.520
we hear it can be
challenging to keep track

25
00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:04.115
of different versions of
the models and their codes.

26
00:01:04.115 --> 00:01:06.350
Different training
procedure parameters,

27
00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:08.780
hyperparameter values
in each trial,

28
00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:11.480
and performance metrics control

29
00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:13.620
the experiments
space to advance.

30
00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:15.860
In every iteration,
these practitioners

31
00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:18.170
need to monitor what
changes are being made,

32
00:01:18.170 --> 00:01:19.895
which ideas are being tried,

33
00:01:19.895 --> 00:01:21.035
which ideas work,

34
00:01:21.035 --> 00:01:22.790
and which ideas don't,

35
00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:25.160
pinpoint the best
performing model when

36
00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:27.635
the models are benchmarked
against each other.

37
00:01:27.635 --> 00:01:30.350
The best model here refers
to the one that delivers

38
00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:33.595
the ideal result for
your specific use case.

39
00:01:33.595 --> 00:01:35.720
Collaborate with
data scientists,

40
00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:39.455
data engineers, ML engineers,
application developers,

41
00:01:39.455 --> 00:01:42.620
site reliability engineers,
business analysts and

42
00:01:42.620 --> 00:01:46.375
business users in
operationalizing the ML models.

43
00:01:46.375 --> 00:01:49.410
Then there's the matter
of reproducibility.

44
00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:50.870
Deploying a model to

45
00:01:50.870 --> 00:01:52.070
a production environment is

46
00:01:52.070 --> 00:01:54.275
difficult unless it
can be reproduced.

47
00:01:54.275 --> 00:01:55.970
In fact, bypassing

48
00:01:55.970 --> 00:01:57.920
the reproducibility
of the model is often

49
00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:01.720
discouraged or disallowed
by policies or regulations.

50
00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:04.340
Reproducibility can
be a major concern

51
00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:06.350
for ML practitioners because

52
00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:07.760
they want to be able to rerun

53
00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:11.260
the best model with a more
comprehensive parameter sweep.

54
00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:13.700
When a team successfully
trained and make

55
00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:16.160
some model ready for production
in a streamlined fashion,

56
00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:19.480
performance, and agility
are considerably improved.

57
00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:22.580
Even if there's a manual
review step in the pipeline,

58
00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:24.500
automation ensures
that each job is

59
00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:27.410
configured and executed
in a repeatable manner,

60
00:02:27.410 --> 00:02:29.780
which reduces the
risk of errors.

61
00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:32.315
Also, for a production
application,

62
00:02:32.315 --> 00:02:33.830
the model needs to be updated

63
00:02:33.830 --> 00:02:35.840
regularly as new data comes in.

64
00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:39.480
Therefore, traceability
becomes paramount.